  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Wednesday) \nIssued at 1202 AM CDT Tue Aug 26 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will shift eastward on Thursday, opening \nthe door for a series of weak shortwave troughs to track southward\nacross the area embedded in northerly flow aloft. This will \nprovide us with the potential for isolated to scattered showers \nand thunderstorms overnight Wednesday through last Thursday night.\nThese storms are not likely to be severe but some gusty winds are \nalways possible with convection during the summer months. With \nthe cloud cover in place and a weak surface boundary pushing \nthrough the area, highs on Thursday will be a little cooler (mid \n90s to near 100). This may be Abilene's chance to break the \nconsecutive 100 degree streak. After that, dry conditions return \nas the high pressure ridge builds back once again. May not be \nhitting 105+ like earlier this week, but highs near or a few \ndegrees above the 100 degree mark look likely into mid next week.",
